[[:db/cas 17592186388911 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2{e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$" "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=2{e}^{x}$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 2?$"] [:db/cas 17592186389084 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=2$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png)" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $2\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=2$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=2$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ad/add2af763d4293bdbcbd7bd86a8e68d9a383269346fb1adbfff22d8e91be165a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389105 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the second and third quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png)" "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the second and third quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186389119 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b077d20bcb79f293383a7810d1c46ad42ce78d17a8d631b01f66a9fd547f475.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" "![](images/6b/6b077d20bcb79f293383a7810d1c46ad42ce78d17a8d631b01f66a9fd547f475.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?"] [:db/cas 17592186389139 :question/problem "![](images/6d/6ddc0b2138025b07ffdb7bf34afabeba578400b194922bec7a733cc9246c7c0f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=2-x,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "![](images/6d/6ddc0b2138025b07ffdb7bf34afabeba578400b194922bec7a733cc9246c7c0f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=2$ and the line $y=2-x,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [:db/cas 17592186389250 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v-1 \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =17 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram." "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v-1 \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-v \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =17 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram."] [:db/cas 17592186389257 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a pentagon." "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u-v \\\\ 4u-v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the pentagon under the action of $T$ is a pentagon."] [:db/cas 17592186389374 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+3y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints  \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-y-z=-1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=-1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨0,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda \\\\ 3 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda &=1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ z &=\\frac{3}{2\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $y$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,z=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $y=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5} & =-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}. \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $y=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=-\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right) & =-1 \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),\\,(-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5})$ | $(-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $4$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),$ and its maximum value is $4.$" "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x+3y+2z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints  \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=2x-y-z=-1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=-1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,3,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨0,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=2\\lambda \\\\ 3 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 2 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu z\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda &=1 \\\\ y &=\\frac{2}{\\mu } \\\\ z &=\\frac{3}{2\\mu }\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $y$ and $z$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{4}{5},\\,z=\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y$ and $z$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $y=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{5} & =-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{5}. \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $y=-\\frac{4}{5}$ and $z=-\\frac{3}{5},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y-z & =-1 \\\\ 2x-\\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right)-\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right) & =-1 \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),\\,(-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5})$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{6}{5},-\\frac{4}{5},-\\frac{3}{5})\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $4$ | $-6$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}),$ and its maximum value is $4.$"] [:db/cas 17592186389421 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}10xy & 5{x}^{2}-2y\\end{bmatrix}$" "Find ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}10xy & 5{x}^{2}-2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390036 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}(2i-1)={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(2i-1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(2i-1)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}(2i-1)={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(2i-1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(2i-1)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}\n$$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186390036 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}(2i-1)={1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(2i-1)={k}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(2i-1)=(k+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}(2i-1)={1}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}(2i-1)={k}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}(2i-1)=(k+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(2i-1)={n}^{2}\n$$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186390353 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{10}{|x-6|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-12x+35}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390474 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-4$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation $$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4.\n$$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-4$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186390531 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}?$" "Which of the following is the graph of  $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390531 :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/f0/f02afa6dd2d1c107fd72b1221fddb7f6c698506deb834cad0dc11af1bebb18e6.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $\\frac{1}{10}$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-\\frac{1}{10},$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{9}{10}).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57539870ff45bae449d3ad7c11580d9c8068cfe76ac98c2f849ee38494288036.png)" "First we plot the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/f0/f02afa6dd2d1c107fd72b1221fddb7f6c698506deb834cad0dc11af1bebb18e6.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $\\frac{1}{10}$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-\\frac{1}{10},$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{9}{10}).$\nThe resulting graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}\\right)}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}\n$$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57539870ff45bae449d3ad7c11580d9c8068cfe76ac98c2f849ee38494288036.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186390545 :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}-3?$" "Which of the following is the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}-3?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390545 :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e6/e648e3a5f0c844d6596d4848629c3e6749647228116bcc5da6fce250895141d5.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/378016bbd17371e3dfee1e80934e76b1357139d405cb320ac5befa252da82b28.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,-\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png)" "We start with plotting the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e6/e648e3a5f0c844d6596d4848629c3e6749647228116bcc5da6fce250895141d5.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}.\n$$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/378016bbd17371e3dfee1e80934e76b1357139d405cb320ac5befa252da82b28.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{1}{2}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{x}-3.\n$$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,-\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186390694 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{31}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -8 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390711 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390718 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}46 \\\\ -23\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}46 \\\\ -23\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}46 \\\\ -23\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 1-6\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{46}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -6 \\\\ -8 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}46 \\\\ -23\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390757 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=3$ and ${y}_{2}=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 3=-\\frac{9}{2}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=3 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=3$ and ${y}_{2}=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=y=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 3=-\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390777 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting into the second equation, we obtain ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=-3.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting into the second equation, we obtain ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}-\\frac{7}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390784 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=-2{e}^{4} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &={e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3}{c}_{1}=3{e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3}(e)+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =e\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{0} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{4} \\\\ {e}^{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &=-2{e}^{4} \\\\ 2{e}^{3}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} &={e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3}{c}_{1}=3{e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{1}=e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3}(e)+{c}_{2}={e}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =e\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}-{e}^{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390791 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${f}_{13}$ in  $F=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What is the minor of ${f}_{13}$ in  $$\n\\displaystyle F=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 3 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390841 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to -2}f(s)$, if $f(s)=⟨(s+3)^{1/(s+2)},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(s+2)}{(s+2)},\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+2s}{{s}^{2}+3s+2}⟩.$" "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to -2}f(s)$, if $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨(s+3)^{1/(s+2)},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(s+2)}{(s+2)},\\,\\frac{{s}^{2}+2s}{{s}^{2}+3s+2}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186390855 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$"] [:db/cas 17592186390881 :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe increment in $y$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method with step size $Δx$ is computed as the weighted average of four sample slopes:\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{′}+2{y}_{1}^{′}+2{y}_{2}^{′}+{y}_{3}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{1}^{′}=f({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{2}^{′}=f({x}_{2},{y}_{2})$ is the estimated slope at $$\n\\displaystyle ({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{′}\\cdot Δx),\n$$\n\n- ${y}_{3}^{′}=f({x}_{3},{y}_{3})$ is the estimated slope at $({x}_{3},{y}_{3})=(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{′}\\cdot Δx).$"] [:db/cas 17592186390920 :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}y(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{4t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-4)t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $Y(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-4)t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Y(s-4),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-4>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+4.\n$$" "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{4t}y(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{4t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-4)t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $$\n\\displaystyle Y(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-4)t}\\,y(t)\\,\\text{d}t=Y(s-4),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-4>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186391043 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-7t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/d6/d604781deed3206caf0a3bf6afa857c00beca9d532a26dedef5c717b4f684225.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391057 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/96/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/20/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/96/96801605aa5d777eb18c13ded1f7b7e59ae74968b210da3b7a26f339a1c9f673.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{7t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{7t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{3t})^{′}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}}{3{c}_{1}{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{7{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/20/20ef7519d0ca517d0d7d06d2998f7bd6c77f04145243f721e197fbef5c087791.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391144 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{9t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/99/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{9t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{9t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/99/99d6e9894c7117c5684e17702540d0eb469b6e824381cf2263c843f8f55ee8d8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{9t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{9t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{9{c}_{1}{e}^{9t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{9{c}_{1}}{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/c4/c497d1f9dd6cb5bc473bd46ec56c465228408447fb2436b80f16a23b282654b2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391151 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdbeba46a0f4640e909b0ba489c1aec1fa87a6aa7740f19d08f274b7351d11b.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/41/4193300af5a8950ce70e2c469812424235665faac279dac0c89289a0307ab46a.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/3f/3fdbeba46a0f4640e909b0ba489c1aec1fa87a6aa7740f19d08f274b7351d11b.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-4{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/41/4193300af5a8950ce70e2c469812424235665faac279dac0c89289a0307ab46a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391158 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/062b58a307f6d40c7be59d527cc01d7d301bd3f0115a0074ba0f3dca54412425.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/062b58a307f6d40c7be59d527cc01d7d301bd3f0115a0074ba0f3d