.$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/b4/b406c864e820819c5ae6f548c7a025a1e1d8197fb785ea5689080034e04210cb.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391187 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/c4/c46568f3173dfeaab6e33e83269262e8692f13e245a42983ba1a9d4302ec5357.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391194 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/a2/a27e83035006f198d454498aafe91624e0576ab43993bde970faf426a9e4cef2.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/a2/a27e83035006f198d454498aafe91624e0576ab43993bde970faf426a9e4cef2.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391226 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0d/0d9e032bfffa68f487347d6c169999e313247e89ef7de3fae28edb216dcf718f.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\nx=0.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0d/0d9e032bfffa68f487347d6c169999e313247e89ef7de3fae28edb216dcf718f.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391286 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/99/99629c2f73e3a10db2ef139848c93c6a2489d995d3d992b7dfb0332989b4f48f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bdfe2e3f89db20665d2c7c1e49c7ddae42387fc1e5bdde8849a9ed1de7e33687.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/99/99629c2f73e3a10db2ef139848c93c6a2489d995d3d992b7dfb0332989b4f48f.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/cb/cb491075c2fecf4aa28f4b167e91ac5a0b7475cf57c1b43eb94e8e1427a28922.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391293 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b3/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/12/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-3x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b3/b39e589968babd6f1417b37dc0f53796ab363ee80ec5fc3860e9793b88b1e98c.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=2x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/12/12a1f8f11c3f1063804220884e6616ae4c9e9aee956adc575de1f0e88e0dd038.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows: \n\n![](images/1b/1b3ed684a9981397191fda952481ff10e114f5f092e83b3defa618c9c8d1cb46.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391394 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -16 & -4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ -4{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =0\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -16 & -4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{4\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ -4{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =0\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186391401 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/40/40b02cfc27ab7bc7bcc4a76523e106066b6ed6f56c184c7bd1f59a0cd1dcfc27.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5c/5c35d385e4ce7496a5fbbcbd344ce04583f7eaf8356e1a76a15625b77df75121.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/40/40b02cfc27ab7bc7bcc4a76523e106066b6ed6f56c184c7bd1f59a0cd1dcfc27.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/5c/5c35d385e4ce7496a5fbbcbd344ce04583f7eaf8356e1a76a15625b77df75121.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186391446 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-2+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186391944 :question/problem "Find the minor of ${c}_{11}$ in \n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 17 & 13 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the minor of ${c}_{11}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 17 & 13 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186391951 :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane?" "What is the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1\n$$ and the plane $y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane?"] [:db/cas 17592186391951 :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $y=2$, we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-2 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the hyperbola: $-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.$" "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $y=2$, we substitute $y=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+1-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =-2 \\\\ -\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the hyperbola: $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186392010 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" "Find an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186392147 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{12}-4{x}^{10}+{x}^{3}-2x-3$, find $f(A).$" "Given the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & 8 \\\\ -24 & -14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $f(x)={x}^{12}-4{x}^{10}+{x}^{3}-2x-3$, find $f(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186392218 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{23}-25{x}^{21}-4x+2$, find $f(A).$" "Given the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $f(x)={x}^{23}-25{x}^{21}-4x+2$, find $f(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186392225 :question/problem "Given the matrix\n$\\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $f(x)={x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6$, find $f(A).$" "Given the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $f(x)={x}^{15}-3{x}^{13}+{x}^{3}-5x+6$, find $f(A).$"] [:db/cas