ion/problem "Find $Pv$ if \n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find $Pv$ if \n$$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393674 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393709 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393716 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & \\sqrt{3} & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393751 :question/problem "Find the second derivative of $f(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,k$ with respect to $t.$" "Find the second derivative of $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,k\n$$ with respect to $t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186393772 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n${i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand \n${j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393779 :question/worked-solution "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n${i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand \n${j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Every linear transformation in ${R}^{2}$ can be represented as a $2\\times 2$ matrix. The standard matrix representation of the transformation has columns that are the images of the vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of the transformation.\n\nFrom the diagram we see that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {i}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {j}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix for the linear transformation is \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393919 :question/problem "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1?$" "What is the center of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393947 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{t},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{t}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:* \n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}+C\n$$" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+4t{y}^{′}+2y={e}^{t},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{c}(t)=\\frac{A}{t}+\\frac{B}{{t}^{2}},\n$$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:* \n\n$$\n\\int t{e}^{t}\\text{d}t=(t-1){e}^{t}+C\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393989 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2-9\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 0 & =-9\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-{90}^{∘},{90}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot (-90)=-8\\,100.\n$$" "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2-9\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 0 & =-9\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=-{90}^{∘},{90}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot (-90)=-8\\,100.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186393996 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$" "Find an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =10.$"] [:db/cas 17592186394078 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+5(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-7+1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 5+1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{w}_{1}-4{w}_{2} &=-2 \\\\ 9{w}_{1}+6{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{w}_{2}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+5(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1-10t){e}^{-t} \\\\ (1+15t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394092 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}10-7 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4-7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{w}_{1}+{w}_{2} &=-1 \\\\ -9{w}_{1}-3{w}_{2} &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{{w}_{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$  we get ${w}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3},$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{1}-\\frac{{c}_{2}}{3} \\\\ 3{c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-3\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{7t}-3\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(1+3t){e}^{7t} \\\\ -9t{e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394112 :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y+1 & 3{y}^{2}-4x-1\\end{bmatrix}$" "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y+1 & 3{y}^{2}-4x-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394119 :question/problem "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "If $v=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186394119 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+2\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2-2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+2\\text{i} & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394154 :question/problem "![](images/c4/c497efbb28bade962b997d7e14f5a272f0cead56f7567b1baaebae695c7c80f4.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=e,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $y={e}^{x},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dA=$" "![](images/c4/c497efbb28bade962b997d7e14f5a272f0cead56f7567b1baaebae695c7c80f4.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $y=e,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $y={e}^{x},$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,dA=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394352 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394359 :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394366 :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+1,$ and the lines $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$ and $y=8-2x.$ Express ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions." "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}+1,$ and the lines $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$ and $y=8-2x.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\n$$ as a sum of repeated integrals over type II regions."] [:db/cas 17592186394497 :question/problem "![](images/8c/8c3056de2634d5315aebcbc978bf279eeb95da736ab6796c9d86dc39d9feadc5.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "![](images/8c/8c3056de2634d5315aebcbc978bf279eeb95da736ab6796c9d86dc39d9feadc5.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [:db/cas 17592186394504 :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+\\frac{1}{2})^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,y\\le 0\\},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394504 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2d/2d50f2375c071d8bfb4c5c58a836bef76a70557c2593d4df9acf48f9f0e7527b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/98/98e9c8442c17cbc77083c5dc55a9d74324befa05e3cd47e1c65c38cd510c08c6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/61/617a69d44661d03e1c170b0c397d11f6437a7222c286df5023154371ec90ab4d.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(-\\frac{1}{2},0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $$\n\\displaystyle {(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/2d/2d50f2375c071d8bfb4c5c58a836bef76a70557c2593d4df9acf48f9f0e7527b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/98/98e9c8442c17cbc77083c5dc55a9d74324befa05e3cd47e1c65c38cd510c08c6.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394545 :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,x\\le 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\le \\frac{1}{4},\\,x\\le 0},\n$$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394545 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/32/32c4bf490df51121ddf1575df8211033185d392fd5b08519971723b820da191f.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/b1/b1e2eac0041d0b0cb802561b3fb83fd8fab1b940cb6c42f698879837c06cbff0.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/19/193601785ff94605d12b91b7cab1e7390af532ba1912530bd8d3f492324e2788.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/32/32c4bf490df51121ddf1575df8211033185d392fd5b08519971723b820da191f.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/b1/b1e2eac0041d0b0cb802561b3fb83fd8fab1b940cb6c42f698879837c06cbff0.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/19/193601785ff94605d12b91b7cab1e7390af532ba1912530bd8d3f492324e2788.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394552 :question/problem "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\le 0},$ then ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A=$" "If $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\\,y\\le 0},$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394627 :question/problem "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}).$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle (\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394654 :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec2b27e99ad03df281464b1a1fb08b5e8cbf869acf1892a815d9031bf506dafe.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the third quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above. What is \n${∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "![](images/ec/ec2b27e99ad03df281464b1a1fb08b5e8cbf869acf1892a815d9031bf506dafe.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the third quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $3$ and $5,$ as shown above. What is \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [:db/cas 17592186394661 :question/problem "![](images/ac/acb2f50d01df6ebec6003cc7b9c944b759113b9f364455fb62eede389b335229.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" "![](images/ac/acb2f50d01df6ebec6003cc7b9c944b759113b9f364455fb62eede389b335229.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=-3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [:db/cas 17592186394786 :question/worked-solution "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "This system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 0-(-5)\\cdot 8}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -8 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394799 :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry."] [:db/cas 17592186394806 :question/problem "${\\int }_{-5}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-5}^{5}(-2{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394813 :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." "Simplify the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry."] [:db/cas 17592186394943 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+3y=x{e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function ${y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{B}{x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}+C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=(x-1){e}^{x}+C\n$$" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+5x{y}^{′}+3y=x{e}^{x},\\,x>0\n$$\n\nhas the complimentary function $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{c}(x)=\\frac{A}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{B}{x},\n$$ find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation. \n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:* \n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6){e}^{x}+C\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\int x{e}^{x}\\text{d}x=(x-1){e}^{x}+C\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186394990 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 1 & =4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ -3{x}_{2} &=-3 \\\\ 9{x}_{2} &=9.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=1.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 1 & =4 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =-1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395093 :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 3=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle {q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{22}=-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 3=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395430 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-6,\\,-2,\\,8⟩$\nand \n${\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,3,\\,-1⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\frac{1}{2}f(t)-g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ \ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-6,\\,-2,\\,8⟩\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,3,\\,-1⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395437 :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t$ given that ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩$ and ${\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{5}{2}⟩.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}[f(t)+4g(t)]\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,\\,\\sqrt{3}-1,\\,2⟩\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}g(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{5}{2}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395444 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,-10,\\,-14⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,-10,\\,-14⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395451 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{2}{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-6,\\,12,\\,-15⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{2}{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-6,\\,12,\\,-15⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395458 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)+⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨5,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)+⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨5,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395472 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-1,\\,-3,\\,5⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨9,\\,-3,\\,-12⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-1,\\,-3,\\,5⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{5}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨9,\\,-3,\\,-12⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395479 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,-2,\\,-8⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,0,\\,-2⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨4,\\,-2,\\,-8⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-3,\\,0,\\,-2⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395609 :question/problem "${\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨2t-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}⟨2t-1,\\,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395630 :question/problem "Find $\\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\int ⟨{e}^{t},\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395637 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{sec}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395644 :question/problem "$\\int ⟨{3}^{t},\\,{csc}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int ⟨{3}^{t},\\,{csc}^{2}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395728 :question/problem "$\\int \\sqrt{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{5x+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395838 :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" "Find an eigenvector of\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395838 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395931 :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{3/2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{4}{15}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{4}{15}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}{t}^{3/2}(\\frac{5}{3}-t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{s}.$" "The particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{3/2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{4}{15}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}(1)^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{5}+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{15} & =\\frac{4}{15}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the moments in time when $v=0\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5/2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}{t}^{3/2}(\\frac{5}{3}-t) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,\\,\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0$, the particle is stationary when $t=\\frac{5}{3}\\,\\text{s}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186395938 :question/problem "Calculate ${Q}^{-1}$, where\n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Calculate ${Q}^{-1}$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186395957 :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $x$-axis." "Calculate the point 