roof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ and\n{{field-3}}." "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L6}$ follows from line $\\text{L5}$ and\n{{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186396502 :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then $F(s)$ is twice differentiable for all $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$  \n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then $F(s)$ is twice differentiable for all $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$ exists for $s>a.$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt\n$$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle {F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$  \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$  \n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}."] [:db/cas 17592186396516 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"170px\">$3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"170px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"170px\">$3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"170px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186396527 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1))\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x))=2y \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x) & 2y \\\\ 2x & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(1,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) & 2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & -\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1))\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is not zero, our system is $\\text{almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186396556 :question/worked-solution "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & -\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & -\\pi (0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ \\pi (1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (0)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (0))\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0-0\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$" "The system can be rewritten in matrix form as \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe need to compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\text{\\boldsymbol}f(x).$ \n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y))=-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y) & -\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi y) \\\\ \\pi y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}J(0,1) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) & -\\pi (0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\ \\pi (1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (0)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (0))\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ \\pi  & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 0-0\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the Jacobian determinant is zero, our system is $\\text{not almost linear}$ at ${x}^{∗}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186396585 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}?$" "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186396620 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{e}^{6i/n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186396680 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].$" "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[2{e}^{(k\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)/n}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{n}].\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186396694 :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}?$" "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 1759218